WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.790
You're on a train, sending 45.00 to a friend from your banking app.

2
00:00:06.790 --> 00:00:09.476
You tap send, and the train enters a tunnel.

3
00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:12.816
Ten seconds later, the app sends the request again.

4
00:00:12.816 --> 00:00:15.687
Out of the tunnel, your balance is 90.00 lower.

5
00:00:15.933 --> 00:00:17.546
And here's the odd part.

6
00:00:17.546 --> 00:00:21.980
Every layer on the way promises to handle each thing exactly once.

7
00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:24.667
So how did one tap move the money twice?

8
00:00:24.667 --> 00:00:25.944
Hold that question.

9
00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:27.319
Here's the path.

10
00:00:27.319 --> 00:00:38.160
The phone sends the request over TCP, the rules that carry bytes between two machines, to the API server, the bank's program that takes requests from apps.

11
00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:42.768
The server writes a record to Kafka, a log that the next program reads.

12
00:00:42.768 --> 00:00:46.029
The program that keeps that log is called the broker.

13
00:00:46.267 --> 00:00:55.191
Then a consumer reads the record, and adds a row to the ledger, the bank's list of every movement of money, kept in PostgreSQL, a database.

14
00:00:55.467 --> 00:00:57.881
Picture posting a present to a friend.

15
00:00:57.881 --> 00:01:02.456
Every depot on the way scans each parcel, so nothing goes through twice.

16
00:01:02.733 --> 00:01:07.432
A week passes, no word, so you post a second one, the same present.

17
00:01:07.432 --> 00:01:09.466
Every depot scans it happily.

18
00:01:09.466 --> 00:01:11.009
New parcel, new label.

19
00:01:11.233 --> 00:01:14.184
Only you and your friend know they're the same gift.

20
00:01:14.467 --> 00:01:14.964
Map it.

21
00:01:14.964 --> 00:01:16.170
You're the phone.

22
00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:18.016
The parcel is the request.

23
00:01:18.016 --> 00:01:21.707
The depots are the layers, and each scan is a guard.

24
00:01:21.707 --> 00:01:23.908
Keep the second parcel in mind.

25
00:01:24.133 --> 00:01:27.820
Now the faults, one per hop, and the guard that lives there.

26
00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:31.507
One: a packet is sent again although the first copy arrived.

27
00:01:31.767 --> 00:01:37.484
TCP numbers every byte on a connection and drops bytes it has already delivered.

28
00:01:37.484 --> 00:01:38.841
That one is caught.

29
00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:46.381
Two: the broker adds the record, but its "got it" is lost, so the server sends it again, with the same sequence number.

30
00:01:46.633 --> 00:01:51.482
The idempotent producer lets the broker spot the repeat and add nothing.

31
00:01:51.482 --> 00:01:53.031
That one is caught too.

32
00:01:53.031 --> 00:01:55.187
Idempotent means safe to repeat.

33
00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:56.341
Now the train.

34
00:01:56.341 --> 00:02:02.795
The server saved the transfer, 80 milliseconds after the tap, and the answer died in the tunnel.

35
00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:05.735
Ten seconds later, the phone sends again.

36
00:02:05.735 --> 00:02:07.670
Which guard catches this one?

37
00:02:07.670 --> 00:02:08.737
Pause and guess.

38
00:02:11.967 --> 00:02:12.891
None of them.

39
00:02:12.891 --> 00:02:17.655
The retry comes on a new connection, and TCP's numbers start fresh.

40
00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:22.406
The server sends a new record, which gets a new sequence number.

41
00:02:22.406 --> 00:02:25.786
Every guard sees a new, perfectly good transfer.

42
00:02:25.786 --> 00:02:26.560
Second row.

43
00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:27.335
90.00 gone.

44
00:02:27.533 --> 00:02:28.151
One more.

45
00:02:28.151 --> 00:02:34.123
The consumer writes the row, then dies before it saves how far it has read, its offset.

46
00:02:34.123 --> 00:02:36.663
On restart, it gets the record again.

47
00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:44.992
Kafka's exactly-once mode saves offsets and Kafka writes together, but the ledger lives in PostgreSQL, outside it.

48
00:02:45.267 --> 00:02:49.519
And the database runs each insert as a fine new transaction.

49
00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:53.275
So each guard is right, but only inside its own layer.

50
00:02:53.275 --> 00:02:56.235
Each one's exactly-once stops at its own edge.

51
00:02:56.667 --> 00:02:58.141
The lab measures it.

52
00:02:58.141 --> 00:03:02.931
1,000 transfers, the same faults, each guard switched on in turn.

53
00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:12.017
With none, the ledger gets 103 extra rows, and 53,025.23 moves that nobody asked to move.

54
00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:14.939
TCP's guard brings it to 78.

55
00:03:14.939 --> 00:03:17.860
The idempotent producer, to 47.

56
00:03:18.133 --> 00:03:21.895
Four careful guards, and every one of them would pass an audit.

57
00:03:22.167 --> 00:03:25.649
The 47 left are lost answers and consumer crashes.

58
00:03:25.649 --> 00:03:32.057
They happen above every layer: one in the phone's retry, one in the gap between two systems.

59
00:03:32.300 --> 00:03:33.472
Back to the present.

60
00:03:33.472 --> 00:03:37.283
You write a reference number on it, and your friend keeps a list.

61
00:03:37.283 --> 00:03:40.917
Here, only the phone knows that two requests are one transfer.

62
00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:47.713
So it makes an id when you tap, req-7f3a, and sends the same id on every attempt.

63
00:03:47.967 --> 00:03:53.989
It rides in a header on the request, then a header on the Kafka record, all the way to the ledger.

64
00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:58.276
There, the insert says: if this id is already here, do nothing.

65
00:03:58.276 --> 00:04:00.927
And the money moves only if a row went in.

66
00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:02.862
The retry: nothing.

67
00:04:02.862 --> 00:04:04.960
The redelivery: nothing.

68
00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:07.321
And the lab's last line: 0.

69
00:04:07.567 --> 00:04:10.019
So are the lower guards useless?

70
00:04:10.019 --> 00:04:10.249
No.

71
00:04:10.249 --> 00:04:16.227
TCP's resending saves you from redoing a whole transfer for every lost packet.

72
00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:18.947
Lower layers make the common case fast.

73
00:04:18.947 --> 00:04:21.143
Correctness is checked at the ends.

74
00:04:21.500 --> 00:04:23.515
And make the id once per tap.

75
00:04:23.515 --> 00:04:27.615
A fresh id on every attempt is a fresh transfer every time.

76
00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:29.067
One quick check.

77
00:04:29.067 --> 00:04:33.372
Kafka's exactly-once mode is on, and there's no request id.

78
00:04:33.372 --> 00:04:37.823
The consumer dies after the insert, before saving its offset.

79
00:04:37.823 --> 00:04:38.990
One row, or two?

80
00:04:38.990 --> 00:04:40.157
Pause and think.

81
00:04:43.367 --> 00:04:43.651
Two.

82
00:04:43.651 --> 00:04:49.844
The ledger's save happened outside Kafka, so only an id checked at the ledger stops it.
